"Colorful Simplicial Depth, Minkowski Sums, and Generalized Gale Transforms (https://academic.oup.com/imrn/article/2019/6/1894/4085561)\nciteturn1185search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.2 years ago; Crawled: last month; Oxford Academic ... International Mathematics Research Notices, Volume 2019, Issue 6, March 2019, Pages 1894–1919, https://doi.org/10.1093/imrn/rnx184 ... In general, even if the origin is contained in the interior of the full-dimensional core, adding several copies of |$0$| to the color classes produces configurations with a much larger colorful simplicial depth than the expected bound. ... If moreover each of the |$A_i$| is affinely independent for all |$0 \\le i \\le d$|⁠, the converse holds: each totally mixed facet is of |$\\sum \\nolimits_{\\mathcal{V}} A$| is of the form |$\\bigcup \\mathcal{V}_i\\setminus\\{v_i\\}$| for a hitting colorful simplex |$\\sigma=\\{v_0,\\dots,v_d\\}$| with |$0\\in\\text{int}G(\\sigma)$|⁠.\n\nVolume 2019 Issue 6 March 2019\n\n\nJournal Article\n\n# Colorful Simplicial Depth, Minkowski Sums, and Generalized Gale Transforms\n\nKarim A Adiprasito ,\n\nKarim A Adiprasito\n\nEinstein Institute for Mathematics, Hebrew University of Jerusalem, Jerusalem, Israel\n\nSearch for other works by this author on:\n\nOxford Academic\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\nPhilip Brinkmann ,\n\nPhilip Brinkmann\n\nFachbereich Mathematik und Informatik, Freie Universität Berlin, Berlin,Germany\n\nSearch for other works by this author on:\n\nOxford Academic\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\nArnau Padrol ,\n\nArnau Padrol\n\nInstitut de Mathématiques de Jussieu—Paris Rive Gauche (UMR 7586), Sorbonne Universités, Université Pierre et Marie Curie (Paris 6), Paris, France\n\nCorrespondence to be sent to: e-mail: [email protected]\n\nSearch for other works by this author on:\n\nOxford Academic\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\nPavel Paták ,\n\nPavel Paták\n\nEinstein Institute for Mathematics, Hebrew University of Jerusalem, Jerusalem, Israel\n\nSearch for other works by this author on:\n\nOxford Academic\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\nZuzana Patáková ,\n\nZuzana Patáková\n\nEinstein Institute for Mathematics, Hebrew University of Jerusalem, Jerusalem, Israel\n\nSearch for other works by this author on:\n\nOxford Academic\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\nRaman Sanyal\n\nRaman Sanyal\n\nInstitut für Mathematik, Goethe-Universität Frankfurt, Frankfurt am Main, Germany\n\nSearch for other works by this author on:\n\nOxford Academic\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\nInternational Mathematics Research Notices, Volume 2019, Issue 6, March 2019, Pages 1894–1919, https://doi.org/10.1093/imrn/rnx184\n\nPublished:\n\n19 August 2017\n\nArticle history\n\nReceived:\n\n03 March 2017\n\nRevision requested:\n\n09 July 2017\n\nAccepted:\n\n14 July 2017\n\nPublished:\n\n19 August 2017\n\nDiscover the most relevant content quickly with our AI Discovery Assistant\n\n[Input]\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe colorful simplicial depth of a collection of |$d+1$| finite sets of points in Euclidean |$d$|-space is the number of choices of a point from each set such that the origin is contained in their convex hull. We use methods from combinatorial topology to prove a tight upper bound on the colorful simplicial depth. This implies a conjecture of Deza et al. [7]. Furthermore, we introduce colorful Gale transforms as a bridge between colorful configurations and Minkowski sums. Our colorful upper bound then yields a tight upper bound on the number of totally mixed facets of certain Minkowski sums of simplices. This resolves a conjecture of Burton [6] in the theory of normal surfaces.\n\n## 1 Introduction\n\nThe classical Carathéodory theorem states that for any point |$p$| in the convex hull of a finite set |$V \\subset \\mathbb{R}^d$|⁠, there is a subset |$S \\subseteq V$| of at most |$d+1$| points with |$p \\in \\text{conv}(S)$|⁠. Of course, this is invariant under translation and for all practical purposes one can always assume |$p = 0$|⁠. In 1982, Imre Bárány [3] gave beautiful colored version of this result.\n\n \n\nTheorem 1.1\n\n(Colorful Carathéodory theorem). Let |$C_0,\\dots,C_d \\subset \\mathbb{R}^d$| be finite sets such that |$0 \\in \\text{conv}(C_i)$| for all |$0 \\le i \\le d$|⁠. Then there is some |$S \\subseteq C_0 \\cup \\cdots \\cup C_d$| with |$|S \\cap C_i| = 1$| for all |$i$| such that |$0 \\in \\text{conv}(S)$|⁠. □\n\nThe sets |$C_i$| are thought of as color classes and |$C = \\{ C_0,\\dots, C_d \\}$| is called a colorful configuration. We call |$C$|centered if |$0 \\in \\text{relint} \\, \\text{conv}(C_i)$| for all |$0 \\le i \\le d$|⁠. The assumption that |$C$| is centered can be considerably weakened (see, e.g., [2, 3, 13, 20]) but in this article we will only consider centered configurations; see Question 1. We call a subset |$S \\subseteq \\bigcup_i C_i$| a colorful simplex if |$|S \\cap C_i| \\le 1$| for all |$i$| and we call the simplex |$S$|hitting if |$\\dim S = |S| - 1 = d$| and |$0 \\in \\text{conv}(S)$|⁠.\n\nInspired by the simplicial depth introduced in [15], Deza et al. [7] introduced the colorful simplicial depth, |$\\text{depth}(C)$|⁠, of a colorful configuration |$C$| as the number of hitting simplices of |$C$|⁠. In particular, they initiated a systematic study of the extremal values of |$\\text{depth}(C)$| as |$C$| ranges over all colorful configurations in |$\\mathbb{R}^d$|⁠. The Colorful Carathéodory theorem asserts that for any centered configuration, there is at least one hitting simplex. For lower bounds on the colorful simplicial depth, Deza et al. confine themselves to the case of colorful configurations in |$\\mathbb{R}^d$| such that the core, |$\\bigcap_i\\text{conv}(C_i)$|⁠, is of full-dimension |$d$| and contains the origin in its interior. In particular, they conjectured that if |$|C_i| = d+1$|⁠, then\n\n\\[ 1 + d^2 \\ \\le \\ \\text{depth}(C).\\]\n\nThe initial lower bound of |$2d$| from [7] was improved in a series of articles [4, 8, 9, 26] culminating in the resolution of the conjectured lower bound by Sarrabezolles [24]. In [7] also a conjectured upper bound was proposed.\n\n \n\nConjecture 1\n\n([7, Conjecture 4.4]). Let |$C = \\{ C_0, \\dots, C_d \\}$| be a centered colorful configuration in |$\\mathbb{R}^d$| with |$|C_i| = d+1$| for all |$0 \\le i \\le d$| and |$0$| in the interior of the core. Then\n\n\\[\\text{depth}(C) \\ \\le \\ 1 + d^{d+1}.\\quad\\square\\]\n\n### 1.1 Upper Bounds on colorful simplicial depth\n\nThe first goal of this paper is to prove this conjecture in the following stronger form. We say that a colorful configuration |$C$| in |$\\mathbb{R}^d$| is in relative general position if no colorful simplex |$S$| of |$C$| of dimension |$d-1$| contains the origin in its convex hull.\n\n \n\nTheorem 1.2\n\nLet |$C = \\{ C_0,\\dots,C_d \\}$| be a centered colorful configuration in relative general position in |$\\mathbb{R}^d$| with |$|C_i| \\ge 2$| for all |$0 \\le i \\le d$|⁠. Then\n\n\\[\\text{depth}(C) \\ \\le \\ 1 + \\prod_{i=0}^d (|C_i| - 1).\\quad\\square\\]\n\nThe relative general position assumption is natural as otherwise for a colorful configuration with |$C_1 = \\{0\\}$|⁠, we would have the trivial upper bound of |$\\prod_i |C_i|$|⁠. In general, even if the origin is contained in the interior of the full-dimensional core, adding several copies of |$0$| to the color classes produces configurations with a much larger colorful simplicial depth than the expected bound. This behavior can happen even if all classes avoid the origin; for example, by adding several copies of a point |$p$| to |$C_0$| and several of |$-p$| to |$C_1$|⁠. This yields exactly |$1 + (n_0 - 1) \\cdots (n_d-1)$| hitting simplices. See Fig. 1 for an example. □\n\n#### \n\nImage: Configuration from Example 1.3 for $\\boldsymbol{\\rm n}=(2,3,4)$.\n\nFig. 1.\n\nConfiguration from Example 1.3 for |$\\boldsymbol{\\rm n}=(2,3,4)$|⁠.\n\nOpen in new tabDownload slide\n\nWe will prove Theorem 1.2 in Section 2 by means of combinatorial topology. More precisely, we show that for centered colorful configurations in relative general position, the number of hitting simplices is related to the reduced Betti numbers of an associated simplicial complex, the avoiding complex. In particular, Theorem 1.2 follows from a result that shows that a certain Betti number is independent of the configuration. To show this, we introduce the notion of flips between colorful configurations, and we prove our result by “flipping” any configuration to the configuration given in Example 1.3.\n\n### 1.2 Colorful Gale transforms of Minkowski sums\n\nThe second goal of this article is to highlight a connection between colorful configurations and faces of Minkowski sums. The Minkowski sum of convex polytopes |$P_0,\\dots,P_s \\subset \\mathbb{R}^d$| is the polytope\n\n\\[P \\ = \\ P_0 + \\cdots + P_s \\ = \\ \\{ p_0 + \\cdots + p_s : p_i \\in P_i \\text{ for } 0 \\le i \\le s \\}\\]\n\nand this operation is key to many deep results in many areas, notably convex geometry [25] and computational commutative algebra (e.g., [10]). The combinatorial complexity of Minkowski sums of polytopes has been subject of several studies [1, 18, 22].\n\nUsing Gale transforms and Cayley embeddings, we define in Section 3colorful Gale transforms associated to a collection |$P_0,\\dots,P_s$| that, similar to ordinary Gale transforms, capture the facial structure of Minkowski sums in the combinatorics of colorful configurations.\n\nA particularly interesting case is that of Minkowski sums of simplices. A face |$F$| of the Minkowski sum |$P$| is of the form |$F = F_0 + \\cdots + F_s$| for faces |$F_i \\subseteq P_i$|⁠. A face is totally mixed if each |$F_i \\subset P_i$| is an inclusion-maximal face, that is, |$F_i$| is a facet of |$P_i$|⁠. If each |$P_i$| is a simplex, then we show in Lemma 3.8 that totally mixed facets are exactly the hitting simplices containing |$0$| in their interior of the associated colorful Gale transform. In particular, this allows us to prove the following.\n\n \n\nTheorem 1.4.\n\nFor |$d_0,\\dots, d_s \\ge 1$| and |$D=d_0+\\cdots+d_s-s$|⁠, let |$P_i\\subset \\mathbb{R}^{D}$| for |$0\\leq i\\leq s$| be |$d_i$|-dimensional simplices whose Minkowski sum is of full dimension |$D$|⁠. Then the number of totally mixed facets of |$P_0+\\cdots+P_s$| is at most\n\n\\[1 + d_0 d_1\\cdots d_s.\\quad \\square\\]\n\n\n\\[L^0_{i_0} \\cap \\cdots \\cap L^s_{i_s},\\]\n\nfor |$i_0,\\dots,i_s = 1,2,3$|⁠. Define |$U(d,k)$| to be the maximum of the number of inclusion-maximal cones for the intersection of |$k$| fans in general position in |$\\mathbb{R}^d$|⁠. Burton conjectured the following.\n\n \n\nConjecture 2\n\n([6, Conjecture 5.5.14]). For |$d \\ge 2$|⁠, |$U(d,d-1) = 1 + 2^{d-1}$|⁠. □\n\nTo see why Theorem 1.4 settles Conjecture 2, let |$P = \\text{conv}(u_1,u_2,u_3) \\subset \\mathbb{R}^d$| be a triangle. The normal fan of |$P$| is the collection of cones |$N_i = \\{ \\ell \\in (\\mathbb{R}^d)^\\ast : \\ell(u_i) \\ge \\ell(u_j) \\text{ for all } j \\}$| for |$i = 1,2,3$|⁠. These are polyhedral cones given as the intersection of exactly two distinct linear halfspaces and hence the intersections |$N_i \\cap N_j$| for |$i \\neq j$| are halfplanes. Thus, every triangle furnishes a fan in the sense of Burton and it is easy to see that every balanced fan arises this way. Thus every collection of fans in relative general position corresponds to a collection of triangles |$P_0,P_1,\\dots,P_s \\subset \\mathbb{R}^d$| in relative general position. The totally mixed faces of the Minkowski sum |$P_0 + \\cdots + P_s$| are exactly the faces that arise as the Minkowski sum of an edge from each |$P_r$|⁠. Every linear function maximizing on such a totally mixed face is thus in the intersection of one leave from each |$P_i$|⁠.\n\n \n\nCorollary 1.5.\n\nThe number of inclusion-maximal cones in the intersection of |$d-1$| fans in |$\\mathbb{R}^d$| is at most |$1 + 2^{d-1}$|⁠. □\n\n### 1.4 Minkowski transforms\n\nWe close in Section 4 with an alternative construction of colorful configurations associated to Minkowski sums via projections of polytopes. These are constructed using McMullen’s transforms [19]. In contrast to the colorful Gale transforms, these Minkowski transforms are realized in lower dimensional spaces but at the expense of not containing information about Minkowski subsums. In particular, their construction uses inequality descriptions of polytopes and not vertex sets, as in the case of colorful Gale transforms.\n\n## 2 An Upper Bound for Colorful Simplicial Depth\n\nIn this section, we will cast the problem of finding tight upper bounds on the colorful simplicial depth into one of combinatorial topology. More precisely, we will relate the bound of Theorem 1.2 to the Betti numbers of certain simplicial complexes (Proposition 2.2). To set the stage, fix some |$\\mathbf{n}=(n_0,\\dots, n_d)\\in \\mathbb{Z}^{d+1}$| with |$n_i\\geq 2$| and define the |$\\mathbf{n}$|-colorful complex as the simplicial complex\n\n\\[\\mathcal{K} \\ = \\ \\mathcal{K}(\\mathbf{n}) \\ := \\ \\mathcal{V}_0 \\ast \\cdots \\ast \\mathcal{V}_d,\\]\n\nwhere |$\\mathcal{V}_i$| is a zero-dimensional complex on |$n_i$| vertices and |$\\ast$| denotes the join. We identify |$\\mathcal{V}_i$| with its set of vertices and we assume that |$\\mathcal{V}_i \\cap \\mathcal{V}_j = \\emptyset$|⁠. This way, the vertex set of |$\\mathcal{K}$| is |$\\mathcal{V} : = \\mathcal{V}_0 \\cup \\cdots \\cup \\mathcal{V}_d$|⁠. The simplices |$\\sigma \\in \\mathcal{K}$|⁠, called colorful simplices, are exactly the subsets |$\\sigma \\subset \\mathcal{V}$| with |$|\\sigma \\cap \\mathcal{V}_i | \\le 1$| for all |$0 \\le i \\le d$|⁠.\n\n\\[\\mathcal{K} \\ \\simeq \\ \\bigvee_{(n_0-1) \\cdots (n_d-1)} \\mathbf{S}^d.\\]\n\nThis proves the claim. ■\n\nThe main connection between the colorful simplicial depth of |$C$| and the topology of |$\\mathcal{A}(C)$| is the following lemma.\n\n \n\nLemma 2.2.\n\nFor |$\\mathbf{n} = (n_0,\\dots,n_d)$| with |$n_i \\ge 2$|⁠, let |$C$| be a centered colorful |$\\mathbf{n}$|-configuration in relative general position. Then\n\n\\[\\text{depth}(C) \\ \\le \\ \\prod_{i=0}^d (n_i-1) + \\tilde{\\beta}_{d-1}(\\mathcal{A}).\\quad\\square\\]\n\n \n\nProof.\n\nLet |$\\mathcal{A} = \\mathcal{A}(C)$| be the avoiding complex of |$C$|⁠. Using the Euler–Poincaré formula, we compute the reduced Euler characteristic |$\\tilde\\chi(\\mathcal{A})$| in two different ways. The avoiding complex |$\\mathcal{A}$| and the colorful complex |$\\mathcal{K} = \\mathcal{K}(\\mathbf{n})$| agree on all faces up to dimension |$d-1$| and |$\\mathcal{A}$| is only missing the hitting simplices |$\\mathcal{H} = \\mathcal{H}(C)$|⁠. Thus, by Proposition 2.1,\n\n\\[\\tilde\\chi(\\mathcal{A}) \\ = \\ \\tilde\\chi(\\mathcal{K}) + (-1)^{d-1} |\\mathcal{H}| \\ = \\ (-1)^d \\prod_{i=0}^d (n_i-1) + (-1)^{d-1} \\text{depth}(C).\\]\n\nOn the other hand, |$\\tilde{\\mathrm{H}}_{k}(\\mathcal{A}) = \\tilde{\\mathrm{H}}_{k}(\\mathcal{K})$| for |$k < d-1$| and from Proposition 2.1 we get\n\n\\[\\tilde\\chi(\\mathcal{A}) \\ = \\ (-1)^{d-1}\\tilde{\\beta}_{d-1}(\\mathcal{A}) + (-1)^d \\tilde{\\beta}_{d}(\\mathcal{A}).\\]\n\nThis yields\n\n\\[\\text{depth}(C) \\ = \\ \\prod_{i=0}^d (n_i-1) + \\tilde{\\beta}_{d-1}(\\mathcal{A}) - \\tilde{\\beta}_{d}(\\mathcal{A}).\\quad \\square\\]\n\n\nLet |$b = \\frac{1}{s+1} (e_0 + \\cdots + e_s)$| be the barycenter of |$e_0,\\dots,e_s$| and consider the affine subspace |$\\Lambda = \\{ (x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^s \\times \\mathbb{R}^d : x = b \\}$|⁠. Then it is straightforward to check that\n\n\\[\\text{conv}( \\mathrm{Cay}_{{\\mathcal{V}}}({A})) \\cap \\Lambda \\ \\cong \\ \\text{conv}\\left(\\sum \\nolimits_{\\mathcal{V}} A\\right).\\]\n\nIn particular, this induces a bijection between faces of |$\\mathrm{Cay}_{{\\mathcal{V}}}({A})$| and |$\\sum \\nolimits_{\\mathcal{V}} A$|⁠. Cayley embeddings have many favorable properties, in particular in relation to triangulations and mixed subdivisions; see [14].\n\n \n\nLemma 3.2.\n\nLet |$\\mathcal{U}=\\mathcal{U}_0\\cup \\cdots\\cup \\mathcal{U}_s \\subseteq \\mathcal{V}$| be such that |$\\mathcal{U}_i \\neq \\emptyset$| for all |$0 \\le i \\le s$|⁠. Then |$\\mathcal{U}$| is a face of |$\\mathrm{Cay}_{{\\mathcal{V}}}({A})$| if and only if |$\\mathcal{U}_0\\times \\cdots\\times \\mathcal{U}_s$| is a face of |$\\sum \\nolimits_{\\mathcal{V}} A$|⁠. □\n\nLet |$G$| be a Gale transform of |$\\mathrm{Cay}_{{\\mathcal{V}}}({A})$|⁠. By construction, |$G$| is a colorful point configuration and we refer to it as a colorful Gale transform. If |$A$| is a configuration of |$N = |\\mathcal{V}| = |\\mathcal{V}_0| + \\cdots + |\\mathcal{V}_s|$| points in |$\\mathbb{R}^d$|⁠, then |$G$| is a colorful configuration of |$N$| points in |$(N - d - s - 1)$|-dimensional space. Since |$\\mathcal{V} \\setminus \\mathcal{V}_j$| is a face for all |$0 \\le j \\le s$|⁠, it follows from Lemma 3.1 that |$0 \\in \\text{relint} \\, \\text{conv}G(\\mathcal{V}_j)$| and hence |$G$| is actually a centered configuration. This allows us to construct a dictionary between the facial structure of |$\\sum \\nolimits_{\\mathcal{V}} A$| and properties of the colorful configuration |$G$|⁠. We start with a characterization that shows that every centered colorful configuration can be thought as a colorful Gale transform.\n\nA converse statement also holds, for the adequate notion of general position of Minkowski sums of simplices. For example, if all the simplices are in relative general position in the sense of [1], then their colorful Gale dual is in relative general position. We do not elaborate further because we will only need Corollary 3.7 in this direction.\n\nWe are interested in the face structure of |$\\sum \\nolimits_{\\mathcal{V}} A$|⁠. Proposition 3.4 allows to easily translate many properties between the two setups; in particular, information on facets. Notice that, since facets of |$A$| are inclusion-maximal faces, the corresponding sub-configuration of |$G$| is an inclusion minimal subset containing the origin in its convex hull. In particular, facets of |$\\sum \\nolimits_{\\mathcal{V}} A$| correspond to subsets |$\\mathcal{S}=\\mathcal{S}_0\\cup\\cdots\\cup \\mathcal{S}_s$| with |$\\mathcal{S}_i\\varsubsetneq \\mathcal{V}_i$| such that |$G(\\mathcal{S})$| is affinely independent and |$0\\in \\text{relint}(G(\\mathcal{S}))$|⁠.\n\nWe call a facet of |$\\sum \\nolimits_{\\mathcal{V}} A$|totally mixed if |$\\mathcal{U}_i$| is a facet of |$A_i$| for all |$0\\le i\\le s$|⁠.\n\n \n\nLemma 3.8.\n\nLet |$\\mathcal{V} = \\mathcal{V}_0 \\cup \\cdots \\cup \\mathcal{V}_d$| with |$n_i = |\\mathcal{V}_i| \\ge 2$|⁠, and let |$G:\\mathcal{V} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^d$| be a centered colorful configuration that is the colorful Gale transform of |$A : \\mathcal{V} \\to \\mathbb{R}^{n - 2d -1}$|⁠, where |$n=n_0+\\cdots+n_d$|⁠. Let |$\\sigma=\\{v_0,\\dots,v_d\\}$| be a hitting colorful simplex of |$G$| such that |$0\\in\\text{int}G(\\sigma)$|⁠. Then |$\\bigcup \\mathcal{V}_i\\setminus\\{v_i\\}$| is a totally mixed facet of |$\\sum \\nolimits_{\\mathcal{V}} A$|⁠.\n\nIf moreover each of the |$A_i$| is affinely independent for all |$0 \\le i \\le d$|⁠, the converse holds: each totally mixed facet is of |$\\sum \\nolimits_{\\mathcal{V}} A$| is of the form |$\\bigcup \\mathcal{V}_i\\setminus\\{v_i\\}$| for a hitting colorful simplex |$\\sigma=\\{v_0,\\dots,v_d\\}$| with |$0\\in\\text{int}G(\\sigma)$|⁠. □\n\nObserve that the reciprocal does not hold in the general case, since such a totally mixed facet |$\\sum \\nolimits_\\mathcal{U} {A}$| exists if and only if each |$A(\\mathcal{V}_i)$| is a pyramid with basis |$A(\\mathcal{U}_i)$|⁠. However, the case in which we are most interested in is the Minkowski sum of simplices, in which every facet is a pyramidal base.\n\nIn particular, in light of Corollary 3.7, centered colorful |$\\mathbf{n}$|-configurations in relative general position in |$\\mathbb{R}^d$| can be interpreted as the Cayley embedding of a collection of simplices |$A_0,\\dots,A_{d}$| in |$\\mathbb{R}^D$|⁠, where |$D=1+\\sum (\\text{dim}(A_i)-1)$|⁠. The dimensions are not arbitrary: the only possibility that a generic collection of polytopes in |$\\mathbb{R}^D$| can have a totally mixed facet is that |$D=1+\\sum (\\text{dim}(A_i)-1)$| (and then such a facet is isomorphic to a product of facets of each |$A_i$|⁠).\n\nThen Theorem 1.4 is just a result of translating Theorem 1.2 to Minkowski sums.\n\n \n\nProof of Theorem 1.4.\n\nLet |$A : \\mathcal{V} \\to \\mathbb{R}^D$| be the point configuration consisting of the vertex sets of |$P_0,\\dots,P_s$|⁠, and let |$G$| be its colorful Gale transform. The identity |$D=d_0+\\cdots+d_s-s$| implies that |$G$| is |$s$|-dimensional. By Lemma 3.8, the number of totally mixed facets of |$\\sum \\nolimits_{\\mathcal{V}} A$| is the number of hitting simplices of |$G$| that contain the origin in its relative interior. Since this is an open condition, the maximum is clearly attained by a colorful configuration in general position, and the corollary follows directly from Theorem 1.2. ■\n\n \n\nExample 3.9.\n\nWe continue studying the configurations presented in Example 3.5 and Figure 5. Only the first and last examples are in relative general position. Theorem 1.4 states that the sum of two triangles in |$\\mathbb{R}^3$| has at most |$5$| totally mixed facets (of the form edge plus edge). This is the number of totally mixed facets of |$T+T_1$|⁠, whereas |$T+T_3$| only has four. This can be read from the corresponding colorful Gale transforms. The avoiding complex of the first colorful Gale transform consists of two paths on three vertices. It has hence reduced Betti numbers |$\\tilde{\\beta}_0=1$| and |$\\tilde{\\beta}_1=0$|⁠. In contrast, the colorful Gale transform of |$T+T_3$| consists of a four-cycle and an independent edge. Its reduced Betti numbers are |$\\tilde{\\beta}_0=\\tilde{\\beta}_1=1$|⁠. Note how the four cycle witnesses the “missing” totally mixed facet. □\n\nIf there is no face |$\\widehat F \\supset F$| such that |$\\pi(\\widehat F) = F'$|⁠, then all the |$\\lambda_i$| must be strictly positive, and hence the origin is in the relative interior of |$\\text{conv}\\,G_P(I(F))$|⁠.\n\nFor the converse, note that if there are |$\\lambda_i > 0$| for |$i \\in I(F)$| such that |$\\ell = \\sum_{i \\in I(F)} \\lambda_i \\ell_i$|⁠, then |$F$| is the inclusion-maximal face that maximizes |$\\ell$|⁠. Moreover, if |$\\ell$| is in the kernel of |$\\phi$|⁠, then, by definition, it is in the image of |$\\pi^\\ast$| and hence |$\\ell = \\pi^\\ast(\\ell')$| for some |$\\ell' \\in (\\mathbb{R}^e)^\\ast$|⁠. That is, |$\\pi(F)$| maximizes |$\\ell'$| over |$\\pi(P)$|⁠. ■\n\nThe following example shows the connection to usual Gale transforms.\n\n \n\nExample 4.2.\n\nFor |$n \\geq 1$| fixed, let |$\\Delta \\subset \\mathbb{R}^{n-1}$| be the |$(n-1)$|-dimensional simplex with vertices |$v_1,\\dots,v_{n-1},v_n= - (v_1 + \\dots + v_{n-1})$|⁠. Its |$n$| facet-defining linear inequalities |$\\ell_i(x) \\le 1$| satisfy |$\\ell_i(v_j) = 1$| if |$i \\neq j$| and |$<1$| otherwise. If we write |$F_J = \\text{conv}(v_i : i \\in J )$| for the face corresponding to a subset |$J \\subseteq [n]$|⁠, then the convention above yields |$I(F_J) = [n] \\setminus J$|⁠.\n\nFor a given polytope |$Q = \\text{conv}(q_1,\\dots,q_n) \\subset \\mathbb{R}^d$| on |$n$| vertices whose barycenter is the origin, there is a canonical surjective linear projection |$\\pi : \\mathbb{R}^{n-1} \\to \\mathbb{R}^d$| that takes |$v_i$| to |$q_i$| for all |$1 \\le i \\le n$|⁠. The corresponding |$\\Delta$|-transform is then a point configuration |$G_{\\Delta} : [n] \\rightarrow (\\mathbb{R}^{n-1})^\\ast / \\text{im}\\,(\\pi^\\ast) \\cong \\mathbb{R}^{n-1-d}$| and Lemma 4.1 yields that for any |$J \\subseteq [n]$| we have that\n\nThen Lemma 4.1 yields that |$F_0 + \\cdots + F_s$| is a proper face of |$P_0 + \\cdots + P_s$| if and only if\n\n\\[0 \\in \\text{relint} \\, \\text{conv} M(I(F_0) \\cup \\cdots \\cup I(F_s)).\\]\n\nNote that |$M$| is a colorful configuration in |$(d_0+\\cdots+d_s-d)$|-dimensional space and thus typically of much lower dimension than the colorful Gale transform of Section 3. However, as we pointed out in Section 3, the colorful Gale transform also contains information about subsums |$\\sum_{i \\in I} P_i$| for any |$I\\subseteq[s]$|⁠, and in particular, of each of the individual summands. This is not true anymore for the Minkowski transform |$M$|⁠. In general, the number of points in the two transforms does not coincide either: The Minkowski transform is indexed by facets and the colorful Gale transform by vertices.\n\nYet, these two approaches are strongly related. Consider the case where |$P_i \\subset \\mathbb{R}^d$| is an |$(n_i-1)$|-dimensional simplex for all |$0 \\le i \\le s$|⁠. If |$\\mathcal{V}_i$| and |$\\mathcal{F}_i$| index the facets and the vertices of |$P_i$|⁠, respectively, then |$|\\mathcal{V}_i|=|\\mathcal{F}_i| = n_i$| and we can identify both by matching each facet with the only vertex it does not contain. Then, as in Example 4.2, for |$J_i\\subset\\mathcal{V}_i$| the face |$F_{J_i}=\\text{conv}(q_j\\ \\colon\\ j\\in J_i)$| belongs to the facets indexed by |$I(F_j)=\\mathcal{F}_i\\setminus J_i$|⁠.\n\n"
